x = 0 \\):\n\nWhen \\( x = -x \\):\n\\[ f(x) + f(-x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\right) + \\arctan (-x) + \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x}{1 - x} \\right). \\]\n\nKnowing that \\(\\arctan (-y) = -\\arctan (y)\\), we simplify the equation:\n\\[\nf(x) + f(-x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\right) - \\arctan x - \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x}{1 - x} \\right). \n\\]\nHere, the \\(\\arctan x\\) terms cancel out:\n\\[\nf(x) + f(-x) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\right) - \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x}{1 - x} \\right). \n\\]\n\nUsing the identity for the difference of arctangents,\n\\[\n\\arctan (a) - \\arctan (b) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{a - b}{1 + ab} \\right),\n\\]\nwe obtain:\n\\[\nf(x) + f(-x) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{\\left( \\frac{(1 - x)^2}{1 + x} \\right) - \\left( \\frac{(1 + x)^2}{1 - x} \\right)}{1 + \\left( \\frac{(1 - x)^2}{1 + x} \\right) \\left( \\frac{(1 + x)^2}{1 - x} \\right) } \\right). \n\\]\n\nNeglecting the details of the algebraic manipulation shows that this function should have a minimum value at \\( x = 0 \\), since the terms cancel out at \\( x = 0 \\):\n\n\\[ \\arctan x + \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\right) \\]\n\nAlso being symmetric around \\( x = 0 \\):\n\n\\[ f(0) = \\arctan(0) + \\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 - 0}{1 + 0} \\right) = 0 + \\arctan 1 = \\frac{\\pi}{4}. \\]\n\nThe range of the function appears to be from \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) (as a continuous and bounded interval):\n\nConclusion:\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\left[ -\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right]}\\).",
